\pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ DSealect}}${\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ DSealect}}}{ }{\b\fs28 DSealect Help Menu
\par }{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \b {General Program Information
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {
\par DSealect is a PC based program to evaluate and predict the sealing capacity of cementing jobs using the finite element program DIANA. }{The name DSEALECT stands for Diana SEAlant seLECTion.}{
\par 
\par DSealect is a tool aimed at assisting the well engineer to select among several cementing s
cenarios the sealant material that meets the structural and sealing requirements for the considered well. The selection is achieved by carrying out mechanical analyses of the structural behavior of the completed well at several points along the well (anal
ysis points) and for several scenarios concerning the choice of the sealant material (cement scenarios). DSealect allows easy comparison of the performance of several sealants for a specified well configuration. Note that DSealect
 is concerned with primary cementing only, i.e. DSealect is not designed to model plug-back jobs or squeeze cementing.
\par 
\par The sealant used in a primary cementing job, i.e. cement placed in the annulus between the casing and the formation, has three main functions: 
\par - maintain the casing in place,
\par - protect the casing from corrosion,
\par - provide sealing capacity in order to avoid annular fluid migration.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s22\widctlpar\tqc\tx4320\tx4860\tqr\tx8640\adjustright \fs20 {Failure of the sealing capacity can occur due to three phenomena:
\par - radial cracking of the cement sheath, which may allow radial and vertical fluid migration,
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {- debonding at cement faces, , which may allow vertical fluid migration,
\par - and shear deterioration in the cement, which may allow pervasive fluid migration due to a significant increase 
\par   of the material permeability.
\par 
\par DSealect is able to predict the risk of damage associated with the mechanical and thermal loads that a completed well has to withstand over its entire life span. Four types of damage can be identified:
\par - debonding at cement faces: 
\par }\pard \li708\widctlpar\adjustright {
Loss of mechanical bond at one of the cement faces, i.e. either at the cement/casing interface, or at the cement/formation interface. Debonding results in an annular opening which can be detrimental to sealing capacity, dep
ending on the extent of the debonding opening, the pore pressure and the viscosity of the migrating fluid.
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {- shear deterioration in the cement or formation:
\par }\pard \li705\widctlpar\adjustright {Irreversible loss of cohesion of the material under the combination of compressive and shear stresses. \tab 
Shear deterioration appears in various guises: distributed damage by micro-cracking, crushing, scaling in prismatic shells, or localized slip in shear bands. Shear deterioration can be detrimental to sealing capacity depending on the level of strain l
ocalization, the pore pressure and the migrating fluid viscosity.
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {- cracking in the cement:
\par }\pard \li709\widctlpar\adjustright {Fracture planes developing in the cement due to tensile stresses exceeding the material tensile strength. Radial cracks can be detrimental to sealing capacity, depe
nding on the extent of crack opening, the pore pressure and the viscosity of the migrating fluid. In DSealect
, only radial cracking is reported as the two other types of cracking are not relevant. Cross-sectional cracking (horizontal cracking for vertical 
wells) is not detrimental to sealing capacity, and circumferencial (hoop) cracking cannot occur as the bond strengths of the cement at the casing and at the formation walls are assumed to be lower than the cement tensile strength.
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {- plastic deformation in the casing:
\par }\pard \li708\widctlpar\adjustright {\f4 Permanent deformation or straining taking place in the casing. The deformation remains after the load has been removed}{\i\f4 .}{
 Moderate plastic deformation in the casing can result in larger mechanical loads being applied onto the cement sheath potent
ially leading to sealant failure. Large plastic deformation in the casing can result in casing failure. However, the user should note that DSealect
 is not designed to investigate casing collapse modes, which generally result from abnormally high movements in the formations.
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {
\par In order to assess potential sealant damage, DSealect simulates the entire history of the well, including borehole drilling, casing operation, cementing job, cement curing, completion and testing, production and injection, and extreme o
perations like high-rate killing, fracturing, unloading or evacuation. Three types of loads are considered to act on the well:
\par - shrinkage or expansion of the cement during curing,
\par - mechanical fluid pressures inside the casing,
\par - thermal shocks inside the casing.
\par 
\par The mechanical problem is solved using finite element analysis, that is a numerical procedure for analyzing structures and continua. Solution is obtained by discretization of space into a mesh made of nodes and elements. Evolution over time and d
istribution over space of the deformation, stresses, strains and heat flow are solved at the nodes and interpolated over the elements. Finite elements produce approximated numerical solutions of mechanical problems. The approximation error vanishes upon m
esh refinement. In DSealect
, a finite element analysis is carried out for each combination of chosen analysis point along the well and cement scenario. The finite element model used is a 2D plain strain model representing a cross-section perpendicular to the well.
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s1\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \b {DSealect Graphical User interface
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {
\par DSealect has a graphical user interface consisting of a main frame containing a menu bar, a tool bar, a status bar and three interactive sub windows:
\par - the tree structure to the left of the screen, 
\par - the input box area in the center of the screen, 
\par - and the display area to the right of the screen.
\par 
\par The menu bar includes all the general tasks as file manipulation, views, actions and help. The tool bar makes the most current tasks easily accessible. The status bar displays the status of the current project.
\par 
\par The tree structure provides an overview of the project steps and allows to access relevant input forms and display windows by clicking on the relevant leaf. The tree structure is hierarchic, inform
ative and secured. It is hierarchic in the sense that input/output actions are grouped according to their engineering content and that the order of the tree follows the project flow. It is informative in the sense that leaves with completed associated act
i
ons are identified by a colour code (blue text). It is secured in the sense that leaves requiring prior completed actions in upper leaves are displayed but not accessible. In this way the user is explicitly guided through the various project steps without
 
losing flexibility. Modification of existing input data is allowed in all accessible leafs. However, inconsistent input entry or inconsistent modification is not permitted. Completeness of current set of input data is constantly checked. Running the FE an
alysis is not allowed before the project is saved and the input data is complete.
\par 
\par The input box area contains the input boxes, option buttons and menus corresponding to the active leaf. The width of the input box area can be minimised to extend the space allocated to the display area.
\par 
\par The display area shows graphical illustrations of input data or output results. The content of the display area depends on the active leaf. For input steps, it shows relevant graphs and figures illustrating the choices made i
n the input boxes area. The display area is automatically refreshed upon entry/modification of input data. For output steps, overview tables, listed results and graphs are shown. Presentation of the results can be tuned using buttons and option lists.

\par 
\par Mat
erial libraries for cements, formation materials and casings are managed in floating windows that are called from the input area or the Action menu. The material library window allows to browse through the existing material items, create new items, edit a
nd modify existing items and delete items. Material libraries are stored in databases with ~.mdb extensions. Default material libraries are delivered with DSealect
. The user can create his own material libraries. Accessing new libraries can be done through the menu Action/option.
\par 
\par Previous/Next buttons allow vertical displacement in the tree structure, i.e. move to the previous/next input area following the order of the tree structure. Previous/next buttons are provided both in the tool bar (vertical arrows)
 and at the bottom of each input area. The next buttons are disabled when the next input boxes area is not accessible, due to the fact that some data is not complete in previous input areas. The previous button is always enabled, except when in the first 
input area.
\par 
\par The unit conversion combo box in the tool bar allows, at any point during the input stage, to switch between SI, US and Well unit systems. When unit is changed, all input data already entered are automatically converted in all input areas.
\par 
\par DSealect provides a contextual help. When the \ldblquote what\rquote s this?\rdblquote 
 button of the tool bar is pushed on, placing the cursor over input boxes or other input items makes a yellow text box appear next to the cursor. This text box contains the definition and the assumptions made in DSealect regarding the input item. 
\par 
\par 
