Help on the detailed results form. In this form output results can be further investigated using charts, tables, graphs and plots.
\par \cell }\pard \widctlpar\intbl\adjustright {\row }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ HIDD_FormationDefinition}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright 
\fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Formation definition}}${\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ Formation definition}}}{ }{\b\fs28 Formation definition 
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {In this form the parameters with respect to the topography of the formations at the drill site are specified.
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {
\par A formation is defined as a geological layer or lithologic unit, i.e. a bed or deposit composed throughout of substantially the same kinds of rock.
\par 
\par In DSealect, the assumption 
is made that, at the vertical of the drilling site, the topography of the formations can be entirely defined by their top TVD and their bottom TVD, i.e. formations are locally approximated to horizontal layers with constant-thickness. 
\par 
\par The model range is 
defined as the range of depths which defines the region of interest with respect to sealing leakage. The user has only to give detailed definition of the formations, which are located at depths at which sealing leakage is likely to occur. The group of for
mations lying above the model range is termed overburden. The formations lying below the model range do not need to be considered.
\par 
\par Onshore definition: 
\par }{\f4 Drilling of the well is taking place on land. }{In DSealect, fluid static pressures are then deduced taking into account the elevation of the drill floor above the ground.
\par }{\f4 
\par Offshore definition: 
\par Drilling of the well is taking place on a submerged location, e.g. a continental shelf. }{In DSealect, fluid static pressures are then deduced taking into account the elevation of the drill floor above the sea surface, and the depth of the seawater.

\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Mean ground level: 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {For onshore drilling, the mean ground level is the vertical distance betwee
n the mean ground surface and the derrick floor, i.e. the work floor of the derrick where the kelly is connected to the top extremity of the drill pipe.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Mean sea level: 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {For offshore drilling, the mean sea level is the vertical distance between the mean sea surface and the derrick floor.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b TVD (True Vertical Depth):
\par }{Depth measured vertically from the derrick floor. The reference point is taken as Rig Kelly Bush (RKB).
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {
\par Once the Onshore/Offshore frame and the Model range frame are filled, the user is allowed
 to add and remove freely additional formations below the overburden. When a formation bottom TVD is wrongly entered, the user should remove the erroneous formation and add it again with the correct TVD.\page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain 
\s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ HIDD_InsituStresses}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Insitu Stresses}}${\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ Insitu Stresses}}}{
 }{\b\fs28 Insitu Stresses 
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {In this form the parameters with respect to the state of insitu stresses in each formation are specified.
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {
\par Insitu stresses are the initial state of stress existing in the formation before any human intervention (e.g. drilling, cementing\'85
). Insitu stresses are due to weight of the overlying material, confinement, and past geological history (e.g. tectonic forces). After drilling and during well operation, the (undisturbed) far-field stress state observed in the formation is equal 
to the initial insitu stress state.
\par 
\par In DSealect the vertical insitu stresses are deduced from the densities of the overburden and of the formations. Intensities of principal horizontal insitu stresses are deduced from given stress ratios (relatively to th
e vertical stress). Their orientation is defined from the azimuth made by the maximum principal horizontal stress with the North.
\par 
\par The user can enter insitu stress parameters for each defined formation, including the overburden, by first selecting the appropriate line in the overview table and then filling the corresponding input boxes.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Surface undisturbed temperature:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {Temperature that would prevail at the ground surface if there were no influence from seasonal temperature changes in the atmosphere. This t
emperature can be measured a few meters underground where the atmospheric influence is negligible. For onshore drilling, the user can assume an undisturbed temperature of 16 \'baC/ 60 \'baF. In DSealect
, the undisturbed temperature is used as reference temperature for the geothermal temperature distribution within the overburden and the formations.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Geothermal gradient:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {Slope of insitu temperature distribution over depth. The geothermal gradient is the result of the natural ther
mal diffusive processes taking place inside the crust of the earth. The Geothermal gradient is expressed in [temperature unit]/[length unit]. In DSealect, the geothermal gradient is assumed constant over the entire depth of each formation.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Vertical stress gradient:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {Slope of the vertical insitu total stress distribution over depth. The stress gradient is related to the density of the material considered. The stress gradient is expressed in [stress unit]/[length unit]. In DSealect
, the stress gradient is assumed constant over the entire depth of each formation.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Horizontal stress ratio:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {Ratio between the horizontal insitu total stress and the vertical insitu total stress. The stress ratios are unit less. In DSealect, the stress ratios are assumed constant over the entire depth of each formation.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Azimuth of maximum horizontal stress with North:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {Signed angle between the North and the maximum horizontal principal stress, positive towards the east. In DSealect, the definition range of this azimuth angle is [0, 360[.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Pore pressure:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {Hydrostatic pressure of the pore fluid. In DSealect
, all formations are assumed porous and fully saturated. The user is asked to enter pore pressures at the top and bottom of each formation. Pore pressure distribution inside each format
ion is deduced by linear interpolation. Pore pressure jumps may be defined at the interface between two neighbor formations.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Fully drained/Fully undrained option:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {When the fully drained option is selected for a formation, this formation is assumed to be s
ufficiently permeable to allow quasi-instantaneous fluid flow due to pore pressure communication at the borehole wall. In this case, the elastic material properties attached to the formation, i.e. Young modulus and Poisson ratio, are interpreted as draine
d properties.
\par When the fully undrained option is selected, the formation is assumed to be sufficiently impermeable to consider that no fluid flow takes place inside this formation. In this case, the elastic material properties attached to the formation, i.e
. Young modulus and Poisson ratio, are interpreted as undrained properties, while the cohesion and the friction angle are interpreted as drained properties. DSealect deduces internally the corresponding undrained cohesion and friction angle.
\par 
\par When all insi
tu stress data are entered for all formations, the resulting stress distributions are plotted over the model range in the display area. When formation material properties (next form) are also available, the user can select a TVD by clicking on the chart. 
This results in plotting for the selected TVD a Morh-Coulomb chart of the insitu state of stress with the plastic envelop.
\par \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ HIDD_FormationProperties}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Formation Properties}}${\footnote \pard\plain 
\s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ Formation Properties}}}{ }{\b\fs28 Formation Properties
\par 
\par }{The user can enter material properties for each defined formation in the model range, by first selecting the appropriate line in the overview table and then import a material from the formation library, \ldblquote From library\rdblquote 
 button, or define a new formation material, \ldblquote New\rdblquote  button. The \ldblquote Edit\rdblquote  button allows modifying the current formation material. The \ldblquote To library\rdblquote 
 button allows exporting the current formation material to the formation library.
\par 
\par The material properties are a set of parameters describing the mechanical behavior of a solid. These par
ameters generally encompass the elastic behaviour (e.g. stiffness, Poisson effect), the plastic or damage response under mechanical loading (e.g. shear deterioration) and the response under thermal loading (e.g. thermal expansion, thermal conductivity). I
n DSealect, all solids are assumed to be homogeneous continuous and isotropic (i.e. nondirectional in properties).
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Elasticity:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {Property of a material which is }{\f4 able to return to its original shape after experiencing strain and removal of deforming stress.}{ }{\f4 In DSealect
, solid materials are assumed to behave linear elastically before material damage has occurred. The elastic behaviour of solids is defined by two parameters:
\par - }{\b\f4 Young\rquote s modulus}{\f4 , i.e. ratio between tensile or compressive stress and elongation of a solid stressed in one direction. 
\par - }{\b\f4 Poisson ratio}{\f4 , i.e. absolute ratio of the transverse strain to the axial strain of a long specimen under an axial tensile or compressive stress at its ends.
\par }{
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Volumetric specific heat:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {Heat transfer necessary to increase the temperature of a unit volume of a substance by 1\'b0
 , while subject to constant conditions of volume or pressure. Volumetric specific heat is expressed in [energy unit]/([temperature unit].[volume unit])
\par }{\f4 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Thermal conductivity:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {Quantifies the rate of flo
w of thermal energy through a material in the presence of a temperature gradient, given by the amount of heat transfer across a unit area in a unit amount of time, divided by the negative of the space rate of change in temperature in the direction perpend
icular to the unit area. Thermal conductivity is expressed in 
\par [Energy unit]/([time unit].[length unit].[temperature unit])
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Thermal expansion:
\par }{Increase in size of a solid object that takes place because of an increase in temperature.
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f4 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b\f4 Plasticity:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {Property 
of a body by which it undergoes plastic deformation when the applied stress exceeds a certain value, known as the yield value. Materials like rock or soils, despite behaving in a brittle manner in the tensile regime, display a plastic behaviour when deter
ioration occurs in the compressive/shear regime. In DSealect, the following plastic model is adopted for the formations: Mohr-Coulomb plastic envelop. This plastic model is defined by two parameters:
\par - }{\b Cohesion}{, i.e. theoretical shear strength above which plastic deformation is assumed to occur. Cohesion is expressed in [stress unit]. For Mohr-Coulomb plasticity, cohesion, }{\i c}{, can be deduced from the uni-axial compressive strength, }{\i f
}{\i\sub c}{, and the angle of internal friction, }{\i\f3 \'66}{\i\sub 0}{:
