\par }{-}{\b  Friction angle}{, i.e. angle of internal friction, }{\i\f3 \'66}{\i\sub 0}{, of a material behaving according to Mohr-Coulomb plasticity. The Mohr-Coulomb failure envelope is represented in the }{\f3 \'74}{-}{\f3 \'73}{
 space as a straight line crossing the }{\f3 \'74\'20}{axis at the value of the cohesion, }{\i c}{, and has a slope of tan}{\fs12  }{\i\f3 \'66}{\i\sub 0}{.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Hardening behaviour:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {Increase or decrease in resistance to hardness produced by plastic deformation. The hardening behaviour of a plastic material is described by a set of hardening variables specifying the evolution of the pl
astic envelope with increasing plastic strain. In DSealect, choice is given between several predefined hardening curves (in the }{\f3 \'73}{-}{\f3 \'65}{\sub p}{ space):
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 -\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls2\pnrnot0\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb -}}\ls2\adjustright {
Linear hardening, i.e. constant apparent incremental stiffness.  The required hardening parameter is the hardening modulus (lower than the Young\rquote s modulus).
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 -\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls2\pnrnot0\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb -}}\ls2\adjustright {
Linear softening, i.e. linearly decreasing cohesion until reaching cohesionless behaviour. The required hardening parameter is the (compressive) fracture energy.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 -\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls2\pnrnot0\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb -}}\ls2\adjustright {Parbolic softening, i.e. parbolic increase 
and decrease of cohesion until reaching cohesionless behaviour. The required hardening parameter is the (compressive) fracture energy.
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {
\par 
\par \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ HIDD_DrillingOperation}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Drilling Operation}}${\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ Drilling Operation}}}{ }{\b\fs28 Drilling Operation
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {In this form the parameters with respect to the topology of the borehole and to the drilling fluid are specified.
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {
\par In DSealect, the well path within the model range is defined as a straight line. However, the well path above th model range, i.e. in the ove
rburden, is not specified. The only required input data is the length of the well path above the model range so that TMD values can be defined.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b TMD (True Measured Depth):
\par }{Depth/length measured following the well path from the derrick floor.
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b TMD at overburden bottom:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {length of the well path above the model range.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Well inclination:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Angle made by the well line and the vertical. In DSealect, the definition range of the well inclination is \line 
[0, 180]. Inclinations above 90 degrees denote an upward drilling direction.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Azimuth with North:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Signed angle between the projection of the well direction on the horizontal plane and the North direction, positive towards the East. In DSealect
, the definition range of the well azimuth is [0, 360[. Note that the well azimuth can only be defined if the well inclination is different from 0 degrees.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Borehole:
\par }{Wellbore. Cylindrical hole made by drilling or boring.
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Drilling fluid:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Fluid, usually mud, used to prop the well during the drilling operation. In DSealect, the weight of
 the drilling fluid can be defined either using a density definition or a stress gradient definition. Too light a drilling fluid weight can lead to borehole instability and ovalisation or caving of the borehole. DSealect
 is not designed to compute optimum mud weights. The optimum mud for the well to be studied using DSealect
 should be determined using other design tools, possibly computer programs. The user should be aware that if the plastic option is chosen for the formation material properties, inappropr
iately low mud weights can lead to numerical instability (which reflects the mechanical borehole instability) and further result in computation failure.
\par 
\par 
\par \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ HIDD_CasingOperation}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Casing Operation}}${\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ Casing Operation}}}{ }{\b\fs28 Casing Operation
\par 
\par }{In this form the parameters with respect to the casing material, casing shape and casing placement are specified. The casing operation consists in running the casing down the borehole.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Casing:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Steel pipe placed in the well as drilling progresses to provide a means of extracting/injecting fluids during the operational regime of the well.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Percentage eccentricity:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Ratio between the observed eccentricity and the maximum eccentricity, expressed in percent. The eccentricity is the distance measuring the deviati
on from concentric placement of the casing within the borehole. Casing eccentricity is usually due to imperfect casing placement. The less centralizers are used and the more inclined the well is, the more eccentric the casing is likely to be.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Guide shoe depth along hole:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {
Guide shoe TMD. The guide shoe is a short, heavy, cylindrical section of steel filled with concrete and rounded at the bottom, which is placed at the end of the casing string. It prevents the casing from snagging on irregularities in the b
orehole as it is lowered. In DSealect, analysis points along the well can only be entered at TMDs above the guide shoe TMD.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Casing properties:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {The user can enter material properties for the casing by importing a material from the casing library, \ldblquote From library\rdblquote 
 button, or by defining a new casing material, \ldblquote New\rdblquote  button. The \ldblquote Edit\rdblquote  button allows modifying the current casing material. The \ldblquote To library\rdblquote 
 button allows exporting the current casing material to the casing library.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Elasticity:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Property of a material which is }{\f4 able to return to its original shape after experiencing strain and removal of deforming stress.}{ }{\f4 In DSealect
, solid materials are assumed to behave linear elastically before material damage has occurred. The elastic behaviour of solids is defined by two parameters:
\par - }{\b\f4 Young\rquote s modulus}{\f4 , i.e. ratio between tensile or compressive stress and elongation of a solid stressed in one direction. 
\par - }{\b\f4 Poisson ratio}{\f4 , i.e. absolute ratio of the transverse strain to the axial strain of a long specimen under an axial tensile or compressive stress at its ends.
\par }{
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Volumetric specific heat:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Heat transfer necessary to increase the temperature of a unit volume of a substance by 1\'b0
 , while subject to constant conditions of volume or pressure. Volumetric specific heat is expressed in [energy unit]/([temperature unit].[volume unit])
\par }{\f4 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Thermal conductivity:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Quantifies the rate of flow of thermal energy through a material in the presence of a temperatu
re gradient, given by the amount of heat transfer across a unit area in a unit amount of time, divided by the negative of the space rate of change in temperature in the direction perpendicular to the unit area. Thermal conductivity is expressed in 
\par [Energy unit]/([time unit].[length unit].[temperature unit])
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Thermal expansion:
\par }{Increase in size of a solid object that takes place because of an increase in temperature.
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\f4 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b\f4 Plasticity:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Property of a body by which it undergoes plastic deformation when the applied 
stress exceeds a certain value, known as the yield value. Steel is well known to behave plastically both in the tensile and compressive regime.  In DSealect
, the following plastic model is adopted for the casing: Von Mises plastic envelop. This plastic model is defined by one parameter: }{\b yield strength}{, i.e. uni-axial stress at which steel starts to deform plastically.
\par \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ HIDD_CasingStresses}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Casing Stresses}}${\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ Casing Stresses}}}{ }{\b\fs28 Casing Stresses
\par 
\par }{In this form the parameters with respect to the casing initial stresses during the waiting-on-cement phase are specified. The waiting-on-cement phase is the time where structural bond is achieved between cement and casing thanks to slurry hardening

\par 
\par In DSealect, two options are possible for assessing the distribution of }{\b axial stresses}{ along the casing:
\par - }{\b Automatic option}{. Stresses are derived from static equilibrium of the casing, assumed to be filled with displacement fluid and to be floating in slurry and mud.
\par - }{\b Advanced option}{. Stresses are derived from the hook load and the weight per unit length of the casing.
\par In both cases, all vertical loads acting on casing are assumed to be concentrated at the top of the casing string (hook load) or at the bottom (buoyancy force, guide shoe additional weight, formation support at shoe\'85
). This amounts to neglecting frictions of viscous fluids, scratchers, centralizers etc\'85
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Hook load:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Load supported by the hook, i.e. device fixed in the derrick from which the casing is suspended. Hook load is expressed in [force unit].
\par 
\par The initial }{\b cross-sectional stresses}{ are deduced from the distributions of outer and inner pressures on the casing. For the inner pressure, the user can specify if a work string is present.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Work string}{:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Tubing run into the casing and used during cementing to circulate cement slurries, displacement fluids, etc\'85
 The work string acts as a buffer absorbing mechanical and thermal shocks and protecting the casing integrity. At its lower extremity, the work string is fixed to the casing with packers, which provide sealing of the A-annulus.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b A-annulus:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Space enclosed between th
e work string and the casing internal wall. Its function is to protect the casing from mechanical and thermal shocks, which may occur inside the work string. The A\_
annulus can be filled with fluid or gas depending on requirements of mechanical and thermal insulation.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Displacement fluid:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {
Fluid, usually drilling mud or salt water, used during cementing for circulation purposes. The displacement fluid is pumped into the well after the cement to force the cement out of the casing and into the annulus. When no 
work string is used, the displacement fluid is pumped directly into the casing. When a work string is used, the displacement fluid is pumped into the work string and the A-annulus is filled either with the original drilling }{\b mud}{ or with a }{\b 
new fluid}{.
\par 
\par 
\par \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ HIDD_CementingOperation}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Cement Operation}}${\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ Cement Operation}}}{ }{\b\fs28 Cement Operation
\par 
\par }{In this form the parameters with respect to the fluid weights and placement during the waiting-on-cement phase are specified.
\par 
\par During the cementing operation, a slurry of cement and water is placed in the annulus by circulation of mud, slurry and displacement fluid from the inside (downwards) to the outside (upwards) of the casing.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Pressure form fluids above:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Pressure applied on the top of the cement due to the column of flui
d (or gas) sitting above. If not removed, drilling mud remains between the formation and the casing from the cement top to the derrick floor. This pressure has the unit of [stress].
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Lead &Tail Cement option:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {When the Lead &Tail Cement option is selected, the user can place two cements along the well, otherwise only one cement is used.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Lead cement:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {First cement poured during the cementing job. Once in place in the annulus, the lead cement rests on top of the tail cement.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Tail cement:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Second cement poured during the cementing job. Once in place in the annulus, the tail cement is located below the lead cement.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Top of tail cement TMD:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {TMD at the interface between lead cement and tail cement.
\par \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ HIDD_DepthsForAnalysis}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Depth for Analysis}}${\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ Depth for Ananlysis}}}{ }{\b\fs28 Depth for Analysis
\par 
\par }{In this form the depths along the well for which analysis will be carried out are specified. The user can add and remove points. When points are removed all attached load data will be lost.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Analysis point:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Point a
long the well line for which a (finite element) mechanical analysis is to be carried out. The user should sample points along the well at locations where sealing leakage is likely to be the most critical.
\par 
\par \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ HIDD_CementProperties}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Cement Properties}}${\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ Cement Properties}}}{ }{\b\fs28 Cement Properties
\par 
\par }{In this form the parameters with respect to the material properties attached to various cement scenarios are specified.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Cement scenario:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Trial case for the choice of an appropriate sealant. In DSealect, the user is as
ked, for each cement scenario, to define material properties of the lead and tail cement. The user specifies several cement scenarios, which are then run in turn. Comparative results are shown for all cement scenarios, thus allowing the user to select the
 appropriate sealant.
\par 
\par By defining the number of cement scenarios considered, the user specifies how many cement materials needs to be defined. If the number of cement scenarios is increased, new lines are added in the overview table. Materials then need to
 be attached to the additional lines. If the number of cement scenarios is decreased, the last lines of the overview table are removed and attached materials are lost.
\par 
\par The user can enter material properties for each defined cement, by first selecting the appropriate line in the overview table and then import a material from the cement library, \ldblquote From library\rdblquote  button, or define a new cement material, 
\ldblquote New\rdblquote  button. The \ldblquote Edit\rdblquote  button allows modifying the current cement material. The \ldblquote To library\rdblquote  button allows exporting the current cement material to the cement library.
\par 
\par The material properties are a set of parameters describing the mechanical behavior of a solid. These parameters generally encompass the elastic behaviour (e.g. stiffness, Poisson effect), the pl
astic or damage response under mechanical loading (e.g. shear deterioration) and the response under thermal loading (e.g. thermal expansion, thermal conductivity). In DSealect
, all solids are assumed to be homogeneous continuous and isotropic (i.e. nondirectional in properties).
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Elasticity:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Property of a material which is }{\f4 able to return to its original shape after experiencing strain and removal of deforming stress.}{ }{\f4 In DSealect
, solid materials are assumed to behave linear elastically before material damage has occurred. The elastic behaviour of solids is defined by two parameters:
\par - }{\b\f4 Young\rquote s modulus}{\f4 , i.e. ratio between tensile or compressive stress and elongation of a solid stressed in one direction. 
\par - }{\b\f4 Poisson ratio}{\f4 , i.e. absolute ratio of the transverse strain to the axial strain of a long specimen under an axial tensile or compressive stress at its ends.
\par }{
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Volumetric specific heat:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Heat transfer necessary to increase the temperature of a unit volume of a substance by 1\'b0
 , while subject to constant conditions of volume or pressure. Volumetric specific heat is expressed in [energy unit]/([temperature unit].[volume unit])
\par }{\f4 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Thermal conductivity:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Quantifies the rate of flo
w of thermal energy through a material in the presence of a temperature gradient, given by the amount of heat transfer across a unit area in a unit amount of time, divided by the negative of the space rate of change in temperature in the direction perpend
icular to the unit area. Thermal conductivity is expressed in 
\par [Energy unit]/([time unit].[length unit].[temperature unit])
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Thermal expansion:
\par }{Increase in size of a solid object that takes place because of an increase in temperature.
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\f4 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b\f4 Tensile strength:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Maximum tensile stress that a material can withstand without fracturing. Tensile strength is expressed in [stress unit].}{\f4 
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b\f4 Tensile fracture energy:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Energy required to extend a fracture plane by a unit area, assumed to be a material constant. Tensile fracture energy is expressed in [energy unit]/[area unit].}{\f4 
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b\f4 Plasticity:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {
Property of a body by which it undergoes plastic deformation when the applied stress exceeds a certain value, known as the yield value. Materials like cements, despite behaving in a brittle mann
er in the tensile regime, display a plastic behaviour when deterioration occurs in the compressive/shear regime. In DSealect
, the following plastic model is adopted for the cements: Mohr-Coulomb plastic envelop. This plastic model is defined by two parameters:
\par - }{\b Cohesion}{, i.e. theoretical shear strength above which plastic deformation is assumed to occur. Cohesion is expressed in [stress unit]. For Mohr-Coulomb plasticity, cohesion, }{\i c}{, can be deduced from the uni-axial compressive strength, }{\i f
}{\i\sub c}{, and the angle of internal friction, }{\i\f3 \'66}{\i\sub 0}{:
