\par }{-}{\b  Friction angle}{, i.e. angle of internal friction, }{\i\f3 \'66}{\i\sub 0}{, of a material behaving according to Mohr-Coulomb plasticity. The Mohr-Coulomb failure envelope is represented in the }{\f3 \'74}{-}{\f3 \'73}{ sp
ace as a straight line crossing the }{\f3 \'74\'20}{axis at the value of the cohesion, }{\i c}{, and has a slope of tan}{\fs12  }{\i\f3 \'66}{\i\sub 0}{.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Hardening behaviour:
\par }\pard \widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Increase or decrease in resistan
ce to hardness produced by plastic deformation. The hardening behaviour of a plastic material is described by a set of hardening variables specifying the evolution of the plastic envelope with increasing plastic strain. In DSealect
, choice is given between several predefined hardening curves (in the }{\f3 \'73}{-}{\f3 \'65}{\sub p}{ space):
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 -\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls2\pnrnot0\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb -}}\ls2\adjustright {
Linear hardening, i.e. constant apparent incremental stiffness.  The required hardening parameter is the hardening modulus (lower than the Young\rquote s modulus).
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 -\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls2\pnrnot0\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb -}}\ls2\adjustright {Linear softening, i.e. linearly dec
reasing cohesion until reaching cohesionless behaviour. The required hardening parameter is the (compressive) fracture energy.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 -\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls2\pnrnot0\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb -}}\ls2\adjustright {
Parbolic softening, i.e. parbolic increase and decrease of cohesion until reaching cohesionless behaviour. The required hardening parameter is the (compressive) fracture energy.
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ HIDD_CompletionAndTesting}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Completin & Testing}}$
{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ Completion & Testing}}}{ }{\b\fs28 Completion & Testing
\par 
\par }{In this form the parameters with respect to the mechanical and thermal loads applied between completion and shut-in are specified. Completion loads are defined locally at each analysis point by selecting the appropriate line in the overview table.

\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Well type:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {To allow DSealect to set up Production & Injection form and the Extreme Operations form, the user is asked to specify the well type. Seven different well types are suggested:
\par - }{\b Oil producer}{, i.e. well for which oil production is the normal operational regime. In DSealect, an oil producer is allowed to be converted to an injector at a later stage of its life.
\par - }{\b Gas producer}{, i.e. well for which gas production is the normal operational regime.
\par - }{\b Gas storage well}{, i.e. well used to inject and produce gas into/from a porous formation for storage purposes.
\par - }{\b Water injector}{, i.e well for which water injection is the normal operational regime. In DSealect, a water injector is allowed to be converted to a producer at a later stage of its life.
\par - }{\b Steam injector}{, i.e. well for which steam injection is the normal operational regime.
\par - }{\b Thermal well}{, i.e. well for which hot water production is its normal operational regime.
\par - }{\b Water source}{, i.e. well for which water production is its normal operational regime. In DSealect, a water source is allowed to be converted to an injector at a later stage of its life.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Completion:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {
Operation during which work on the well is finished and the well is brought to productive status. This operation generally includes perforation. Perforation is a procedure in which holes are made in the casing opposite the producing formation to allow oil
 or gas to flow into the well, or to allow water to be forced out into the formation, causing hydraulic fracturing. Depending on the technique used for perforation, the pressure existing inside the casing during completion can be lower than the bottom hol
e pore pressure, i.e. }{\b underbalanced completion}{, equal to the pore pressure, i.e. }{\b balanced completion}{, or larger than the pore pressure, i.e. }{\b overbalanced completion}{. In DSealect
, when underbalanced completion or overbalanced completion option is chosen, the user is asked to enter the }{\b pressure difference}{
, i.e. the absolute value of the difference between the completion pressure inside the casing and the bottom hole pore pressure.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Shut-in:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {Period of time during which the wellhead valves have been closed, ceas
ing production. The bottom-hole pressure then gradually becomes equal to the pressure of the fluids that exist in the formation at the bottom of the well. In DSealect
, if the shut-in option is selected, it is assumed that, after completion and testing, the
 well is shut in so that bottom-hole pressure and temperature equilibrium is achieved before the well is subjected to any further loading. If the shut-in option is not selected, operational loads and extreme loads are applied directly after the end of com
pletion.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Pressure test:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {
Shoe bond test. Test applied after curing and before operational regime, which consists in pressurizing the well until a requested equivalent density to check the bonding properties at the weakest points of the structure (generally the casing shoe).

\par 
\par \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ HIDD_ProductionAndInjection}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Production & Injection}}${\footnote \pard\plain 
\s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ Production & Injection}}}{ }{\b\fs28 Production & Injection
\par 
\par }{In this form the parameters with respect to the mechanical and thermal loads applied during the normal operational regime are specified. Production and 
injection loads are defined locally at each analysis point by selecting the appropriate line in the overview table.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Production:
\par }{Operational regime of a well during which the fluids stored in the formation/reservoir are brought to the surface.
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Injection:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {
Operational regime of a well during which a fluid is injected under pressure into the formation/reservoir to supply extra energy for driving the remaining oil/gas toward the production wells, also used in pressure-maintenance operations.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Exposure time to fluid:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {
Total time the well is subjected to a temperature load within one load phase. Heat transfer is a diffusive process occurring over time. The most detrimental temperature distribution for the structure may occur after some time of exposure to a temper
ature load.
\par \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ HIDD_ExtremeOperations}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Extreme Operations}}${\footnote \pard\plain 
\s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ Extreme Operations}}}{ }{\b\fs28 Extreme Operations
\par 
\par }{In this form the parameters with respect to the mechanical and thermal loads applied  during exceptional well operations are specified. Extreme loads are defined locally at each analysis point by selecting the appropriate line in the overview table.

\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b High rate killing:
\par }{Halt oil production in order to recondition a well.
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Fracturing, Stimulation:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {Technique used to increase gas or oil production from a reservoir formation, e.g. hydraulic fracturing, acidizing, acid frac, thermal induced fracturing.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Well unloading:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {Exceptional well operation where the pressure inside the casing is significantly decreased. The most severe case of well unloading is }{\b evacuation}{, where the inside of the casing is kept at atmospheric pressure.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Exposure time to fluid:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {Total time the well is subjected to a temperature load within one load phase. Heat transfer is a diffusive
 process occurring over time. The most detrimental temperature distribution for the structure may occur after some time of exposure to a temperature load.
\par 
\par \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ HIDD_MeshRefinement}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Mesh Refinement}}${\footnote \pard\plain 
\s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ Mesh Refinement}}}{ }{\b\fs28 Mesh Refinement
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {In this form the parameters with respect to the refinement level of the mesh are specified.
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {
\par The user can tune the finite element mesh using sliders. The casing mesh, the cement mesh and the formation mesh can be tuned independently. The more refined the mesh is, the more acc
urate the results, but also the longer the calculation time and the larger the memory requirement. The mesh proposed by default is optimal in most of the cases. In the case of strongly eccentric casings, it is advised to increase the cement mesh refinemen
t.
\par 
\par The user may wish to increase the refinement level in order to capture highly irregular stress/strain fields caused for instance by damage localization. The user should be aware that the calculation time will increase exponentially. The user may wish on
 the contrary to decrease the mesh refinement to reduce time and memory requirements.
\par \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ HIDD_OutputSelection}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Output Selection}}${\footnote \pard\plain 
\s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ Output Selection}}}{ }{\b\fs28 Output Selection
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {In this form the output level is specified.
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {
\par Three output levels are proposed:
\par - the first level selects only essential output, i.e. overview tables and bar charts,
\par - the second level selects also detailed output for the cement,
\par - the third level selects also detailed output for the formation and the casing.
\par \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ HIDD_Analysis}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Analysis}}${\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {
\cs16\super $}{ Analysis}}}{ }{\b\fs28 Analysis
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {In this form an overview of the defined variation cases is given and the finite element analysis can be started.
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {The project must first be saved before calculation is allowed.
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {
\par When the \ldblquote }{\b Run Analysis}{\rdblquote  button is clicked, the calculatio
n window is opened. In the overview table, variation cases can be selected or unselected for analysis using the check boxes of the first column. In the second, third and fourth columns, the following information is provided:
\par - }{\b Run YES/NO}{, i.e. has the variation case ever been run?
\par - }{\b Success YES/NO}{, i.e. if the variation has been run, did the calculation run successfully without encountering numerical divergence?
\par - }{\b Result YES/NO}{, i.e. if the variation has been run, are (complete or partial) results available in the project file?
\par 
\par The fine-tuning of the }{\b stepping scheme}{ and of the }{\b calculation options}{ can be done independently for each variation case by selecting the appropriate line in the overview table.
\par 
\par The}{\b  Stepping Scheme}{ frame allows to tune the parameters
 of the solution procedure. Some particularly unstable analyses result in calculation failure due to divergence of the solution scheme. This problem can usually be solved by adjusting the stepping scheme. Increasing the number of time steps and/or the num
ber of iterations is most of the time sufficient to solve divergence problems. The user should be aware that the calculation time will increase proportionally.
\par 
\par DSealect provides the following }{\b calculation options}{:
\par - }{\b Stop batch run if divergence}{, i.e. if divergence is encountered in one of the variation cases, calculation is stopped and following variation cases are not analyzed.
\par - }{\b Allow further post-processing}{, i.e. the complete set of result, including Femview databases, are kept in the working directory in WorkDir/ProjectName/ProjectName1 \'85
 This option is rather costly in term of memory requirements and is only useful if the user has a Femview license to allow him investigating the results in more detail.
\par - }{\b Save diagnostic}{, i.e. the full analysis diagnostic is saved to the project database and shown in the display window of the Analysis form. 
\par 
\par The \ldblquote }{\b Start}{\rdblquote  button starts the finite element analysis. Analysis progress is monitored in floating windows. Analysis can be aborted at any moment by clicking on the \ldblquote }{\b Stop}{\rdblquote 
 button. If the user answers Yes to the question \ldblquote Abort further calculation\rdblquote 
, all analyses are aborted. If the user answers No to this question, only the current analysis is aborted and calculation resumes with the following variation cases.
\par 
\par The finite element analysis is carried out following a certain number of construction phases and time lines as presented below.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Construction phases:}{
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\i Construction phase 1:
\par }{
The virgin formation is drilled and the borehole wall is subjected to mud pressure. When the casing is run and the slurry is put in place, the casing is floating in fluids and therefore doe not interact mechanically with the formation. In the analysis, sl
urry pressure is also applied on the borehole wall in construction phase 1.
\par }{\i Construction phase 2:
\par }{The casing is put in place floating in drilling mud. No mechanical interaction is present at this point with the formation. The casing is subjected to mud pressure, then to waiting-on-cement fluid pressures.
\par }{\i Construction phase 3:
\par }{The cement is put in place and hardens instantaneously during an isochoric transformation. Therefore, reaction pressures applied on the cement by the formation and the casing are equal to the slurry hydrostatic pressure.
\par }{\i Construction phase 4:
\par }{At the moment of cement hardening, the three well components, i.e. formation, solid cement and casing, are mechanically assembled. From this moment onwards, thermal and mechanical loads can be applied on the entire model.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Branching time line:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {When non-linear irreversible material behaviour is taken into account, as it is the case in DSealect model, the chronology of loading is important. The picture below shows the chronology of loading on branching
 time lines. The branches can be explain as follows:
\par - Before construction phase 4, i.e. assembly of the well components, the formation, casing and cement behave as independent structural components. Construction phases 1 to 3 can therefore be treated as separate time branches.
\par - It is assumed that any well will be submitted to loads from shrinkage to shut-in. Then operational loads are applied in separate time branches as the user is not asked in the GUI to specify the chronology of these loads. This choic
e has been made so that the effect of the different operational loads on the sealing capacity of the well can be assessed separately.
\par 
