\par \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ HIDD_ResultsOverview}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Overview Tables}}${\footnote \pard\plain 
\s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ Overview Tables}}}{ }{\b\fs28 Overview Tables
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {In this form output results are presented in the form of comparative charts and tables.
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {
\par The user can chose the analysis point along the well (TMD) for which he wants to compare the performance of the various cements selected in the cement scenarios. Results are grouped and shown per well component, i.e. sealant, formation or casing.
\par 
\par Two types of results are available:
\par - damage status presented in an overview table with load types in rows and cement scenarios in columns
\par - risk of damage presented in a bar chart with colored bars corresponding to load types and grouped per cement scenario.
\par 
\par Refer to Help sections \ldblquote Completion & Testing\rdblquote , \ldblquote Production & Injection\rdblquote , \ldblquote Extreme Operations\rdblquote  and \ldblquote Analysis\rdblquote  for the definition of the load types.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Damage status:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {The damage status reports the predicted occurrence of damage. All possible damage status are listed in the following:
\par - }{\b intact}{: no damage predicted
\par - }{\b debonding at formation}{: gap opening between cement and formation
\par - }{\b debonding at casing}{: gap opening between cement and casing
\par - }{\b radial cracks}{: radial fracture planes in the cement sheath
\par - }{\b shear deterioration}{ (well component = sealant): plastic strains in the cement sheath
\par - }{\b shear deterioration}{ ( well component = formation): plastic strains in the formation near the borehole wall
\par - }{\b plastic deformation}{ ( well component = casing): plastic strains in the casing
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Risk of damage:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {
Given in term of remaining capacity. The remaining capacity represents the change in stress state necessary to cause damage, i.e. it denotes how far from material failure the stress situation is. It is expressed in % as the ratio between the necessary str
e
ss change from the current state and the necessary stress change from the initial state. 100% correspond to the initial stress state or to a stress state that is safer than the initial stress state. 0% corresponds to onset of damage. The remaining capacit
y can be calculated for each type of damage considered. The remaining capacity presented in this form is the minimum of all damage types.
\par 
\par 
\par \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ HIDD_DetailedResults}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super K}{ Detailed Results}}${\footnote \pard\plain 
\s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super $}{ Detailed Results}}}{ }{\b\fs28 Detailed Results
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {In this form output results can be further investigated using charts, tables, graphs and plots.
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {
\par Results for a given analysis point (TMD) and a given cement scenario can be selected by the user making use of the two first combo boxes. An information box shows the cement material corresponding to the selected analysis point and cement scenario.
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Present result against load & damage type:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {This option allows to show a }{\b damage level overview table}{ or a }{\b risk of damage bar chart}{ in a similar format as in the form \ldblquote Overview Tables\rdblquote . The maximum da
mage level and the minimum remaining capacity within each load type is presented per damage type. The nature of the damage level per damage type is given in the following:
\par - }{\b debonding at formation}{: maximum gap opening between cement and formation in [microns]
\par - }{\b debonding at casing}{: maximum gap opening between cement and casing in  [microns]
\par - }{\b radial cracks}{: maximum radial crack mouth opening in the cement sheath in [microns]
\par - }{\b shear deterioration}{ (well component = sealant): maximum plastic strains in the cement sheath in [microstrain]
\par - }{\b shear deterioration}{ ( well component = formation): maximum plastic strains in the formation in [microstrain]
\par - }{\b plastic deformation}{ ( well component = casing): maximum plastic strains in the casing in [microstrain]
\par 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\b Present result against space or time:
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {
This option allows to select a damage type and investigate its time of occurrence and its spatial distribution within the load phase where it arises first. The load phase of first damage occurrence is given in the information box, }{\b Load type}{
. The option }{\b evolution over load level + time}{ shows a plot of the damage level against the value of the fluid pressure inside the casing and/or against time for thermal loading. The option }{\b distribution over space}{
 shows either contour plots for the distribution of plastic strains, or vector plots for the distribution of radial cracks, or line graphs for the distribution of the debonding gap opening along the cement sheath interface.
\par \page }{\i 
\par }}